WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.120
When one system is breached,
how far does it spread?

00:00:03.495 --> 00:00:05.985
The question is not
whether we get compromised.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:07.655
It is how much.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.080
First, one compromised
system reaches everything.

00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:15.575
On a flat network,
the attacker spreads undetected.

00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:19.995
With segmentation, one hacked
system stays an isolated problem.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.230
Consider network segmentation
and a zero trust architecture.

00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:28.167
Second, one password becomes total access.

00:00:28.572 --> 00:00:32.282
Shared admin credentials ignore
every privilege boundary.

00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:36.772
In minutes the attacker holds domain
admin, and everything is readable.

00:00:37.152 --> 00:00:41.642
Consider tiered admin accounts,
separate devices and no shared passwords.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.740
Third, no one asks for permission.

00:00:45.195 --> 00:00:49.845
Permission creep and forgotten access
mean the attacker inherits everything.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:55.200
Consider least privilege, role-based
permissions and periodic access reviews.

00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:57.527
What good looks like.

00:00:57.812 --> 00:01:01.022
Blast radius is a design choice,
not a consequence.

00:01:01.422 --> 00:01:06.112
Segmentation, tiered admin and least
privilege cost engineering time.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:10.237
A breach that spreads company-wide
costs orders of magnitude more.

00:01:10.527 --> 00:01:11.457
With the controls,

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:13.297
spread stops at one department.

00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:15.572
Hours, not days or weeks.

00:01:15.932 --> 00:01:18.482
The question is not,
can we afford to segment?

00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:20.867
It is, can we afford not to?

00:01:22.663 --> 00:01:23.873
A suggested first step.

00:01:24.158 --> 00:01:27.168
Ask the CIO or the CISO for four numbers.

00:01:27.548 --> 00:01:29.518
Segments with firewalled boundaries.

00:01:29.903 --> 00:01:33.313
High-privilege accounts kept
apart from routine work.

00:01:33.558 --> 00:01:38.168
Data that is classified and restricted,
and access reviews completed.

00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:41.873
Blast radius is a design choice.

00:01:42.228 --> 00:01:43.918
Isolate before you have to.

